        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "e1774141-3282-5a4d-be65-0426d2d9c34d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Sylvia Plath, Ted Hughes, Michael Cunningham, Frieda Hughes, Nicholas Hughes\n- Works: The Bell Jar, The Hours\n- Places: Boston, Smith College, Newnham College, University of Cambridge, United States, England\n- Topics: Poetry, novels, short stories, depression, electroconvulsive therapy, suicide, literary awards\n\nFacts:\n- Sylvia Plath was born on October 27, 1932, in Boston and became an American poet, novelist, and short story writer.\n- Sylvia Plath studied at Smith College and at Newnham College, University of Cambridge, before receiving acclaim as a poet and writer.\n- Sylvia Plath married fellow poet Ted Hughes in 1956, and they lived in the United States and then in England.\n- Sylvia Plath and Ted Hughes had two children, Frieda Hughes and Nicholas Hughes, before separating in 1962.\n- Sylvia Plath experienced clinical depression for most of her adult life and was treated multiple times with electroconvulsive therapy.\n- Sylvia Plath died by suicide on February 11, 1963.\n- The Bell Jar is the only novel written by Sylvia Plath.\n- Michael Cunningham was born on November 6, 1952, and is a U.S. novelist and screenwriter.\n- Michael Cunningham is best known for the 1998 novel The Hours, which won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction and the PEN/Faulkner Award in 1999.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "786f2ed5-7c15-5401-8998-57b23899c959",
        "text": "Page: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: Sylvia Plath ( ; October 27, 1932 – February 11, 1963) was an American poet, novelist, and short story writer.\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: Born in Boston, she studied at Smith College and Newnham College at the University of Cambridge before receiving acclaim as a poet and writer.\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: She married fellow poet Ted Hughes in 1956, and they lived together in the United States and then in England.\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: They had two children, Frieda and Nicholas, before separating in 1962.\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: Plath was clinically depressed for most of her adult life, and was treated multiple times with electroconvulsive therapy (ECT).\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: She committed suicide in 1963.\nPage: Michael Cunningham. Sentence: Michael Cunningham (born November 6, 1952) is a U.S. novelist and screenwriter.\nPage: Michael Cunningham. Sentence: He is best known for his 1998 novel \"The Hours\", which won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction and the PEN/Faulkner Award in 1999.\nPage: The Bell Jar. Sentence: The Bell Jar is the only novel written by the American writer and poet Sylvia Plath.",
